<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coles Creek culture</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Coles Creek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> culture was a Late Woodland archaeological culture of the Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> valley, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and Mississippi, which followed the Troyville culture and is considered ancestral to the Plaquemine culture. Its population grew dramatically, and by 1000 CE simple elite polities had begun to form. From roughly 800 CE its people built flat-topped platform mounds, usually two or three around a central plaza, to carry temples and other civic buildings, often over earlier burial mounds. They lived on acorns and other wild plants and on hunting rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, which only began to increase by 1000–1200 CE, and the bow and arrow came into use.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coles-creek-culture.jpg" alt="Coles Creek culture" width="330" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heironymous Rowe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coles_Creek_culture_map_HRoe_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the lower Mississippi valley the <b>Coles Creek culture</b> was building flat-topped mounds around plazas from about 700 CE, although it still lived mainly by hunting, gathering and native crops.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
